earch\nProjects\nStructured Doctoral Programs\nStructured Doctoral Programs\nCopyright: \u00a9 Martin Braun\nRWTH offers outstanding graduates the opportunity to pursue their doctorates within programs that combine independent research with structured training. These programs provide a financially supported, time-limited, and collaborative environment, giving early-career researchers a reliable framework to advance their projects and develop skills for both academic and non-academic careers.\nOur doctoral programs offer a clear structure and reliable supervision while ensuring intellectual independence. Doctoral candidates work on their research within a well-defined academic framework, supported by a dedicated, interdisciplinary team of professors who work with you to set individual goals and review progress in regular meetings.\nIn addition to their dissertation research, candidates complete a structured curriculum that combines field-specific and methodological courses with transferable skills training. The program also offers optional industry internships or international placements. This comprehensive approach equips you with advanced subject expertise and the key competencies needed for both academic and non-academic career paths.\nThe following doctoral programs are currently offered at RWTH:\nResearch Training Groups and International Research Training Groups\nResearch Training Groups, funded by the German Research Foundation (DFG), combine excellent basic research with structured doctoral training. This approach is designed to prepare for diverse careers in academia and beyond. Open to all disciplines and often interdisciplinary, these groups are supported by universities or technical colleges that have the authority to confer doctoral degrees.\nInternational Research Training Groups extend this successful model through partnerships with leading universities abroad. This gives doctoral candidates the chance to work in a truly global academic environment, expanding their research and career horizons.\nAn overview of the DFG Research Training Groups at RWTH is available on the Research Training Groups page.\nHorizon Europe MSCA Doctoral Networks\nThrough its Doctoral Networks (DN), the European Union supports cross-border doctoral training programs that bring together institutions from both academia and the non-academic sector under Horizon Europe. These programs are designed to enhance career prospects by fostering international, interdisciplinary, and cross-sector mobility, while also strengthening knowledge transfer between partner institutions.\nAn overview of RWTH Aachen University's EU projects, including the Doctoral Networks, can be found in the EU project database.\nOther Doctoral Programs\nRWTH is also involved in other doctoral programs as a coordinator or partner.\nCircular E-Cars - Metal-focused value creation and value retention optimization in the circular economy (BMFTR, coordinator)\nNERD: North Rhine-Westphalian Experts on Research in Digitalization (MKW, partner)\nSusChemSys Doctoral Program (partner)\nSchool for Simulation and Data Science (SSD; JARA CSD)\nMax Planck School Matter to Life (partner)\nMax Planck School of Photonics (partner)\nHelmholtz Graduate School HITEC (partner)\nHelmholtz School for Data Science in Life, Earth and Energy \u2013 HDSLEE (partner)\nTerminated Doctoral Programs\nGraduate College \"Medium-Sized Cities as Co-Participation Cities\"\nNRW Forschungskolleg - ACCESS Welche Mobilit\u00e4t k\u00f6nnen wir uns leisten?\nNRW-Forschungskolleg \u2013 Nachhaltige Energiesysteme im Quartier\nVerbund.NRW \u2013 Ressourceneffizienzsteigerung beim Einsatz von Verbundwerkstoffen und konstruktionen im Bauwesen\nRessourcenKolleg.NRW \u2013 Ressourcengewinnung aus gemischten Abfallfraktionen\nAlternative Nutzfahrzeugantriebe f\u00fcr Lkw und Bus \u2013 Sauberer energieeffizienter Stra\u00dfentransprot (\u201eANFAHRT\u201c)\nSimilar Topics\nThird Party Funding\nFooter\nServices\nContact an